### The Mindset Shift Missing from the AI Conversation
**By Mya** | *Senior/Lead EA & Global AI Program Manager*

---

### The Reality Check: Where My Mindset Actually Started
It’s easy to look at someone’s public work—their frameworks, custom bots, or program leadership—and assume they had it all figured out from day one. 

Let me be completely clear: **I didn’t.**

When Generative AI first landed in my workspace, my initial reaction wasn't a strategic roadmap. It was fear and overwhelm. 

I sat with all the same uncomfortable feelings that so many administrative professionals experience:
* **Fear of obsolescence:** *Will AI replace the EA role? Am I already behind? Did I wait too long to start?*
* **Overwhelm & cognitive fatigue:** *I don't have the bandwidth for this Learning & Development (L&D). I’m rewatching feature trainings multiple times just to grasp basic concepts.*
* **Isolation & friction:** *Feeling under-supported, feeling alone in my anxiety, and worrying that AI would either double my workload or strip away the strategic parts of my job I love.*
* **Quality & capability doubts:** *What if the output isn't good enough? What if these competencies advance faster than my ability to keep up?*

I needed to ask endless questions, lean heavily on my point of contact (POC) network, and either host or attend working sessions just to talk through how a tool actually functioned in practice. I was investing time and energy up front, and for a while, it felt like fear and overwhelm.

---

### The Missing Piece: Culture & Mindset First
During those early, uncertain months, I had countless candid conversations with POCs, engineering partners, and close colleagues across different roles who were embedded in AI work. 

Across industry forums, inter-company committees, and working groups, I noticed a common gap: **everyone was talking about tools, features, and technical execution, but nobody was talking about the human culture shift required to adopt them.**

Across the industry, new software was frequently dropped into non-tech roles' laps with the expectation of seamless adoption, without acknowledging the fear, friction, or sheer mental load of learning a new paradigm. 

I realized then that real enablement doesn't start with a tool demo or a prompt library. **Enablement starts with a conversation around a culture shift and an intentional mindset shift.** Before an EA can effectively build or automate, they need a safe, supported space to reframe *how* they view the technology.

---

### The Anchor Framework: Curiosity, Excitement, Urgency
Once I addressed my own fears and gave myself permission to be a learner, my perspective transformed. I landed on three core anchors that I believe represent the best, most proactive mindset for any EA venturing into AI:

#### 1. Curiosity
Curiosity replaces fear. Instead of asking *"Will this tool replace me?"*, curiosity asks *"What happens if I paste this transcript here? How can this reframe my meeting prep?"* Curiosity gives you permission to experiment, fail, ask "dumb" questions, and rewatch trainings three times without shame.

#### 2. Excitement
Excitement fuels momentum. It’s the thrill of discovering that a workflow that used to take you two hours on a Friday afternoon can now be drafted in two minutes. It’s recognizing that AI isn't here to take your agency away—it’s here to liberate your time so you can focus on high-touch executive partnership, strategic ops, and leadership.

#### 3. Urgency
Urgency provides accountability. Technology moves quickly, and waiting for a perfect, formal company training program means falling behind. A healthy sense of urgency drives you to block 15 minutes on your calendar today, test a feature, reach out to a peer, and take ownership of your own L&D journey.

---

### A Continuous Practice, Not a Fixed State
I want to leave you with one final truth: **this mindset is not something you achieve once and keep forever.**

Even now, leading global programs and managing AI adoption, I don't retain this mindset 100% of the time. There are still days when a new model drops, features change overnight, a build doesn't work, or a heavy EA workload simply causes my L&D time to stall, and that familiar wave of overwhelm creeps back in. 

And that’s okay. 

When those feelings hit, I treat them as a signal to reset, drop the pressure to be an expert, and return to the basics: **Curiosity, Excitement, and a healthy dose of Urgency.**

